<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Triple Alliance (1668)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1668">1668</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-devolution">War of Devolution</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Triple Alliance of 1668 was a league of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, formed in January 1668 to halt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s conquests in the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-devolution">War of Devolution</a>. Negotiated by Sir William Temple and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-de-witt">Johan de Witt</a>, it pushed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to accept the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle in May 1668. It broke up after Charles II secretly allied with France in 1670.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/triple-alliance-1668.jpg" alt="Triple Alliance (1668)" width="320" height="379" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Lely · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir-William-Temple-Bt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Under the 1659 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Treaty of the Pyrenees</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, eldest daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-spain">Philip IV of Spain</a>, who renounced her inheritance rights in return for a dowry of 500,000 gold écus that was never paid. When Philip died in September 1665, leaving his four-year-old son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain">Charles II of Spain</a> as king, Louis argued that the unpaid dowry made the renunciation invalid and that her rights devolved to him under the Jus Devolutionis. He launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-devolution">War of Devolution</a> on 24 May 1667, and by September his troops held much of the Spanish Netherlands.</p><p>After the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-dutch-war">Second Anglo-Dutch War</a> on 31 July 1667, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> began talks on a common front against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Charles II of England blamed Louis for the humiliation of Breda, and Parliament and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">City of London</a> wanted peace. Sir William Temple persuaded the States General to approve the alliance before consulting the provincial bodies, reversing normal practice, so that the French ambassador, Godefroi, Comte d'Estrades, could not delay it with bribes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, which controlled the Baltic trade in pitch and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, signed on 5 May.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The alliance had three parts: a defensive alliance, a guarantee of terms for ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-devolution">War of Devolution</a>, and secret clauses. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> would withdraw from the Spanish Netherlands but keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douai">Douai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tournai">Tournai</a>, Oudenaarde, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kortrijk">Kortrijk</a>, Charleroi and other towns. One secret clause required <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to end its war with Portugal; another committed the allies to force France back to its 1659 boundaries if it fought on. The English Parliament approved £300,000 if needed, and the States General activated 48 warships and the recruitment of 18,000 more troops. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s demand for 480,000 rixdollars was passed on to Spain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>French troops conquered Franche-Comté in February 1668. With the war costing far more than expected, and a secret partition treaty with Emperor Leopold promising him much of the Spanish inheritance should Charles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> die, Louis decided to make peace on terms agreed at Saint Germain in April.</p><p>The alliance did not last: commercial disputes led to the Third Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-dutch-war">Dutch War</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at the outset of the Franco-Dutch War of 1672-1678. Yet it ended the long Franco-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> alliance and was the first step towards the anti-French coalition that lasted until 1714. From 1668 to 1674 Parliament voted large sums for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>, and English diplomacy turned to Sweden, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Triple Alliance (1668)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
